WEBVTT

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:20.290
Fifty years ago, on the hard sands of Daytona Beach, auto pioneers competed in tests of speed

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:21.590
and safety.

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:28.130
And for fifty years, the story of the automobile has been the story of men who dreamed and

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:30.000
worked to prove incredible.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:35.180
competition that they could make the motor car better, safer, faster.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:37.080
They still do.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.180
This is Florida's and the country's greatest automotive show of stock car performance.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:30.000
Air Force Thunderbird over the course in salute as fans of every age pack the grandstand

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.710
and line the world-famous four-mile beach and road course.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.810
This is the first running of this year's cars,

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:41.450
and the fans are waiting to see what the automakers' new models will do

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:45.220
when the chips are down.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.880
The pace lap of the Grand National is underway.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.740
45 stock cars representing almost every American name.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:12.120
What does a man do to turn the automobile you and I drive into a racing stock car, safe and fast enough to compete on the toughest stock car track in the country?

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:20.720
Months before the race, work begins in hometown garages after the day's work is done.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:30.000
Official rules require all engine parts to be standard equipment in production and available from the manufacturers.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:34.690
Only modifications to the body and suspension are permitted

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:37.420
to improve handling and safety in competition.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:40.220
Suspension is reworked and straightened.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:41.950
A straight-through exhaust system

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:46.040
allows the engine to deliver maximum power without back pressure.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:50.740
Anything that adds weight without improving performance is removed.

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:54.520
Headlights, trim, door handles, hubcaps, go.

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:57.540
The passenger side of the front seat goes.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:00.000
The driver's area is padded

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.740
Cushion the driver as he turns corners at 120 miles an hour.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.500
Seat belt and shoulder harness.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:11.920
Hold him in the seat as he bounces along rough straightaways at 140 miles per hour.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:17.160
Roll bars anchored to the chassis will give him breathing space if he flips.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:22.540
The instrument panel is reworked to tell him what he has to know at a glance.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:26.070
Oil, water, fuel, and tachometer.

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:29.290
The engine is tuned and adjusted.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:37.550
now the car is ready for a test run as it moves out of the garage it carries the hopes and dreams

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:54.080
of weeks of work grows in the early morning hours the car is put to the test after the warm-up run

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:56.600
the driver gets the word to open it up

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:13.230
- Run after run, the car is shaken down.

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:16.750
For the final test, it's brought to the beach.

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:19.470
Here's how Paul Goldsmith, top driver,

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:22.920
describes the run around the four mile course.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:26.540
- Why you have a tendency to fish tail and spin

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:30.000
all the way up the entire beach, clear to the north turn.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.360
You get out fairly wide next to the ocean, and you go into a soft sand before you get

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:40.350
to the hard shale and the bank turn, and then your car's pitching and bouncing.

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:45.620
In about the middle of the turn, you broad slide a little bit, and you get a hold of that

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:52.020
blacktop, and your tires squeal for a little ways, and it starts accelerating tremendously

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:53.020
down the backstretch.

00:04:53.020 --> 00:05:00.000
And the speed that you reach on the backstretch, it's in excess of 140 mile an hour.

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:02.380
All four wheels will leave the ground.

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:05.330
That's how rough it is going down through there.

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:08.160
There's one point that I'd say all four wheels

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:10.160
must be, oh, six inches in the air.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:12.160
It feels like when you're in the car.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:16.320
Then you touch your brakes as it's coming down.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.300
Where we drop off into the high bank turn,

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:24.150
which is shale, clay, dirt, sand, and it gets pretty slippery.

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:29.670
You proceed through the turn, and you're coming out on the beach.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:40.770
The time for testing is done.

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:49.250
Race day.

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:54.790
Parade lap of Chevrolets, Mercury, Plymouth, Ford, Pontiac,

00:05:54.790 --> 00:06:00.000
Chrysler, Buicks, Oldsmobile, the field of 45.

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:06.330
wheels off the two-mile beach straightaway and into the north turn.

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:11.900
The next time they hit this turn, they will be shifting down in a second at 120 miles per

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:13.170
hour.

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:18.880
Eighty times, they'll twist through these hairpin turns in a 160-mile race that will

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:26.390
torture chassis and engine more in one afternoon than 50,000 miles on the highway, halfway

00:06:26.390 --> 00:06:30.000
down the asphalt backstretch, and heading for the south turn that will lead

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.100
them back on the beach.

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:37.980
Here they come.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:39.860
They're picking up speed.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:44.000
The green flag.

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:45.210
The race is on.

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:56.370
Move the south turn safely and onto the beach.

00:06:56.830 --> 00:06:58.520
The leaders, jockey for position.

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:00.000
There's trouble in the south turn.

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:04.070
Two cars collide, fighting for the first lap position.

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:09.480
But no one is hurt.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:13.510
Down the beach, full throttle.

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:17.990
Goldsmith and car number three Pontiac is in the lead.

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:21.190
Into the tricky north turn, sliding, braking, shifting,

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:28.090
accelerating in split seconds.

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:30.000
Goldsmith pours on the cold as they

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:38.950
hit the back stretch. Look out! Penny Rakestraw in number 98 Mercury rams the guardrail. He's

00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:44.190
alright, but his car stalled. This may start trouble. The rest of the field make their way

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:49.560
past the disabled car, but watch out! Frank Thompson in number 40 Ford smashes broadside

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:55.340
into Rakestraw, and they both go through the rail. Watch it! One, two, three other cars

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:57.840
lose control, but they stay on the track.

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:03.020
Nobody's hurt, and they're going back for more.

00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:10.930
Dick Foley, a number 61 Chevrolet, spins into the inside rail, and two more cars are out of competition.

00:08:15.350 --> 00:08:18.990
Here comes the leader, Goldsmith, a number three, at the end of the two-mile backstretch.

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:25.190
He dives into the south turn, driving a steady, even pace that's building a lead.

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:30.000
Turner, a number 26 Ford, jams the accelerator to the floor and closes in.

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:34.520
Smokey eunuch, mechanic on number three, signals Goldsmith that Turner is a threat.

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:42.070
And as Goldsmith enters the north turn, he begins to run wide open.

00:08:42.070 --> 00:08:45.170
Turner in Ford number 26 is too close for comfort.

00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:13.220
Goldsmith's average speed is 110 miles per hour and Turner, hot on his tail, cuts the corners making every second count.

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:53.120
alone in the north turn can really make time. Roberts and Buick M1 and Phil Ohr in a Chevrolet

00:09:53.120 --> 00:10:00.000
both lose control and slide broadside around the north turn. Roberts missed Ohr by inches and they're both

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:08.390
the running. Goldsmith's number three is still holding on to first place. Turns out of control.

00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:21.740
He slides through the safety markers and comes to a stop. He recovers and guns the car back on

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:30.000
the course. What a show of car handling. He's not through. There's the white flag. Only one more lap

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.750
to go. Can number three Pontiac

00:10:32.750 --> 00:10:34.330
hold the lead for one more lap?

00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:37.110
The fans are on their feet as number

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:39.050
26 Ford roars after the leader.

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:47.640
Goldsmith misses a turn.

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:54.550
Goldsmith gets back onto the track

00:10:54.550 --> 00:10:56.490
with Turner only yards behind him.

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:00.000
Goldsmith streets for the checkered flag

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:05.680
wins the closest race ever on on Daytona's beach and road course. Before the

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:11.430
year is out, drivers and mechanics will race stock cars 2,000 times on dirt and

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:15.830
asphalt tracks around the country, proving their experience and equipment in

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:22.360
competition. Here's what Smokey Eunuch, winning mechanic, has to say. Well in this

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:30.000
bin is you you have to use about the best that you can get a hold of everything. In the ring department

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:39.200
we can get about any kind of ring we want. Perfect Circle seems to make the best ring for us fellows to race on.

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:47.540
Now we travel from Daytona, with its colors, sight and sound,

00:11:47.540 --> 00:11:53.910
to another scene of glory, to another proving ground.

00:11:53.910 --> 00:11:59.890
Time goes by and as it passes,

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:11.180
"Here comes the final toughest test, here the finest come to battle, and the victory's to the best."

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:19.300
Yes, victory to the best, for only the best, run here at the Indianapolis 500.

00:12:19.300 --> 00:12:22.760
From the time the Speedway opened, race morning at 5 o'clock,

00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:29.600
200,000 people from every state in the Union and 18 foreign countries have been pouring through the gates.

00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:38.320
This once a year spectacular fills the grandstands that frame the track with fans who have reserved their seats months in advance.

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:46.220
Purdue University Band parades the colors along the main straightaway.

00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:52.450
The music, the crowds, the cars are all part of one great show.

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:15.260
rhythms of the band beat out the last few minutes of a year of waiting for the

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:20.270
drivers. For Al Keller, 38 years old, fourth year at Indianapolis. For Paul

00:13:20.270 --> 00:13:27.680
Russo, 44 and a grandfather, 12th year at the 500. Unser, 26, rookie. Thompson, 36.

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:29.960
Sixth year, Cheeseburg.

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:31.110
31.

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:32.260
Second year.

00:13:32.260 --> 00:13:33.180
Goldsmith.

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:34.170
30.

