00:17:44.890 --> 00:17:51.410
The smashed radiator may mean he's out of the race for good.

00:17:51.410 --> 00:17:54.300
What is left of number 97, Dick Grafman's car,

00:17:54.300 --> 00:17:56.060
is towed clear of the track.

00:17:56.060 --> 00:18:00.000
Number 97 is one of eight cars that were totally wrecked and

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:10.660
are out of the race. Gene Marcinat, mechanic for the Novi, is ready to call it quits.

00:18:10.660 --> 00:18:15.490
But driver Russo calls for tools. He wants to get back in the race.

00:18:41.230 --> 00:18:43.180
still a way back. For others,

00:18:43.180 --> 00:18:47.920
Bruce's radiator is repaired

00:18:47.920 --> 00:18:51.120
and he's ready to go. He pulls back

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:53.120
into the race in last place without

00:18:53.120 --> 00:18:55.320
a chance to win, but the

00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:57.110
determination to go the distance.

00:19:01.690 --> 00:19:07.240
After 18 caution laps, the track is clear and the green flag signals for high speed.

00:19:07.240 --> 00:19:12.900
Brian in car number one is in first, Beppenhausen in number 33 is second, Eddie Sachs is third.

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:25.570
And Sachs tried it out down the back stretch.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:35.800
Bentonhausen has hauled up on Brian, and entering the fourth turn, he stands on it and charges into first place.

00:19:37.350 --> 00:19:41.680
Here they come down the front straightaway. There's a new threat.

00:19:42.110 --> 00:19:45.150
Amick, a rookie, in yellow number 99, has taken third.

00:19:48.150 --> 00:19:51.470
Bentonhausen is pushing the pace to 141 miles per hour.

00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:55.160
As the leaders battle their way around the oval, Brian's crew waits.

00:19:55.160 --> 00:19:59.540
Can their car run in front? Only Brian has the answer.

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:05.520
Out of the fourth turn, Brian jolts the throttle of his Belong special and passes Bettenhausen on the outside.

00:20:06.430 --> 00:20:11.540
Rookie Amick comes up fast on the inside, passes Bettenhausen, and makes his bid for first.

00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:16.020
Brian, riding high on the groove, dips down to hold the lead.

00:20:21.730 --> 00:20:28.520
The dogfight for front position is between the three cars in the field with the new low silhouette.

00:20:33.710 --> 00:20:35.870
Coming on to the main straight, it's Amick out in front.

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:47.530
And low on the inside, noses of head of Amick into the turn.

00:20:50.120 --> 00:20:54.210
But Amick, riding faster in the groove, comes into the second turn on top.

00:21:03.290 --> 00:21:12.090
Bettenhausen, also in a car of new design, is crowding Brian for second place.

00:21:12.090 --> 00:21:16.410
Brian and Bettenhausen charge down the main straightaway inches apart.

00:21:16.410 --> 00:21:18.780
But where's Amick?

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:24.450
At 118 miles, the terrific pace has brought Amick, the first of the leaders, into the pits.

00:21:24.450 --> 00:21:30.000
Starting in 25th position, Amick has had to charge all the way to run in front, a sensational

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:33.110
personal effort by a driver in his first 500 try.

00:21:33.110 --> 00:21:38.800
With aiming to the pits, Johnny Boyd in his flight number 9 takes over third place.

00:21:38.800 --> 00:21:40.760
Jim Bryan wheels into the pits.

00:21:40.760 --> 00:21:46.130
He gives Bettenhausen first place while he makes a precautionary tire change and refuel.

00:21:46.130 --> 00:21:57.650
Bettenhausen is driving hard to widen the gap.

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:02.310
Brian wants to get out and go.

00:22:02.310 --> 00:22:09.540
The Ballon Special pulls out with three fresh tires and fuel in 29 seconds.

00:22:09.540 --> 00:22:11.770
Where's Bettenhausen?

00:22:11.770 --> 00:22:16.740
Now, Bettenhausen gives the lead back to Brian to make his pit stop.

00:22:30.890 --> 00:22:45.080
brian rockets off the fourth turn and heads for the main straightaway 40 seconds 45 seconds

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:50.110
and bettenhausen finally gets away can he get back on the track before brian comes around

00:22:50.110 --> 00:23:00.000
as he guns out of the pits brian roars by to hold first place still in third but moving closer four

00:23:00.640 --> 00:23:05.940
puts it in the bow special Brian and Bettenhausen in a fierce duel for the

00:23:05.940 --> 00:23:25.970
lead Boyd now only seconds behind terrific

00:23:25.970 --> 00:23:30.000
pace brings Boyd into the pit his plan had been to run the fire

00:23:30.060 --> 00:23:32.500
with one less stop than the competition.

00:23:40.420 --> 00:23:43.060
Brian and Bettenhausen blister the track

00:23:43.060 --> 00:23:45.320
and precious seconds tick away for Boyd.

00:23:51.340 --> 00:23:53.810
37 seconds, and Boyd pulls out,

00:23:54.080 --> 00:23:55.500
still holding third place.

00:24:02.010 --> 00:24:17.960
he has a job to do to catch the front runners are the lonely mile after the first pit stop 125 miles

00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:28.170
down 375 to go the fans settle back to watch the hours take their toll it's just a matter of time

00:24:30.730 --> 00:24:32.860
This is the test.

00:24:32.860 --> 00:24:35.680
The challenge of nerve and stamina.

00:24:35.680 --> 00:24:40.240
The next 150 laps grind more rubber into the brick and asphalt,

00:24:40.240 --> 00:24:42.370
burn more fuel supplies down,

00:24:42.370 --> 00:24:46.160
drain more strength from weary muscle.

00:24:46.160 --> 00:24:48.570
Through the monotonous, brutal miles,

00:24:48.570 --> 00:24:51.360
the leaders set a torturous pace.

00:24:51.360 --> 00:24:55.120
After three hours, only 14 cars still running.

00:25:02.010 --> 00:25:05.190
Bob Christie and Mike McGill lose control.

00:25:05.190 --> 00:25:08.000
12 cars now.

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:11.120
The leaders pit for the last time.

00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:12.480
Less than two minutes.

00:25:12.480 --> 00:25:14.910
Separate the first four positions.

00:25:14.910 --> 00:25:17.210
Jimmy Rathman, Dick's brother, comes into the pits

00:25:17.210 --> 00:25:24.330
too fast and spins on the apron.

00:25:24.330 --> 00:25:29.080
He decides to go around again.

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:30.000
The last laps.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:39.480
on one. Crew members lean over the pit wall to watch and worry. There's a fire in the pit. Spilled

00:25:39.480 --> 00:25:47.430
fuel burns in the cockpit of car number 59. The safety crew works frantically to smother the flame

00:25:47.430 --> 00:25:55.280
burning only inches away from the fuel tank. M.C. Wilson has to get out. The car is through for the day.

00:25:57.680 --> 00:25:59.380
Only two more laps to go.

00:26:00.030 --> 00:26:08.870
brian is still holding on to first place amic only 30 seconds behind is turning his fastest

00:26:08.870 --> 00:26:23.190
laps in the race void is 12 seconds behind him there's a white flag pointing at brian

00:26:23.190 --> 00:26:27.560
the last lap only one more lap to go

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:42.340
Here comes Bryan down the straightaway for the checkered flag, and he wins the 42nd Indianapolis 500.

00:26:46.850 --> 00:26:50.590
Rookie Amick finishes second, Johnny Boyd finishes third.

00:26:52.260 --> 00:26:59.960
To run in front and stay the distance is the challenge, not only to the men and machines, but to the industry which services.

00:27:01.370 --> 00:27:03.850
Here's what Jimmy Bryan has to say.

00:27:03.850 --> 00:27:07.530
This is the real test. This is like it is for so many things.

00:27:07.530 --> 00:27:11.340
Naturally, the best product gives the best service and

00:27:11.340 --> 00:27:14.480
that's what we need here at Indianapolis to seal all this

00:27:14.480 --> 00:27:19.010
high-powered combustion and be durable to last a full 500 miles.

00:27:19.010 --> 00:27:21.730
And that's why we use Perfect Circle Piston Ring.

00:27:21.730 --> 00:27:28.000
It's the same ring that any customer can buy off the package shelf in any parts store.

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:29.860
Jimmy Bryan,

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:41.630
won $105,000, his share of $305,000, the biggest purse in 500 history. This is the dream come

00:27:41.630 --> 00:27:52.750
true. From this testing and improving come the cars we drive today, and the new cars of tomorrow

00:27:53.280 --> 00:28:00.000
are already on the way. Looking onward to the future,

00:28:00.830 --> 00:28:14.470
As new goals are made and set, still the challenge lies before us, and the challenge will pass.
